<section>
  <h2>Three Wrong Conclusions</h2>

  <p class="lede">Each of these was stated confidently and was wrong. They are recorded because
  the shape they share is more useful than any of them individually: a convenient explanation,
  arrived at quickly, that survived because nobody tested it.</p>

  <h3>One — a silent search result read as an absence</h3>

  <p>An early search for two bug records returned nothing, and the conclusion drawn was that
  the previous session had skipped its own logging discipline. Both records were present — they
  were the top two entries in the file.</p>

  <p>The search tool had skipped the file entirely. It classifies files containing control
  bytes as binary and declines to read them, returning the standard "no matches" exit status.
  Not a warning, not an error: an answer indistinguishable from a genuine absence. The file
  contained control bytes because the entry describing a defect about control bytes had been
  written using them.</p>

  {{ c.warn('The compounding part', 'That made the entire bug log unsearchable by the tool any reader would reach for — while project documentation directs readers to consult it first. The fix restored searchability; the lesson is that a tool reporting nothing and a tool declining to look are the same observation.') }}

  <h3>Two — a lucky streak read as a repair</h3>

  <p>After destroying and rebuilding the container image and dependency cache, the suite passed
  four consecutive times. This was reported as evidence that something had genuinely changed,
  supported by a probability calculated from the prior failure rate.</p>

  <p>Two further runs failed immediately. The calculation had invented a rate from three
  observations and assumed independence between runs that visibly come in streaks. At the true
  rate, four clean runs in a row is uncommon rather than extraordinary — and the number lent
  false confidence to a conclusion that was simply wrong.</p>

  <p>The same error nearly repeated later: reordering the lanes produced four clean runs and
  looked like a fix. It was labelled inconclusive that time, and the mechanism found afterwards
  confirmed it could not have been a fix at all.</p>

  <h3>Three — coverage claimed from incomparable runs</h3>

  <p>Three-engine coverage was reported on the basis of two engines run on the host and one in
  a container. The project already had a written rule against exactly this — all engines run in
  one container so that no engine is special-cased — and its own measurement history records
  mixed execution as a superseded method whose findings were withdrawn.</p>

  <p>The rule was recorded in a measurement report rather than in the project's main
  instructions, which said only that the container was needed for the one engine that cannot
  run natively. That is where it now says every engine goes through the container, and why.</p>

  <h3>What these have in common</h3>

  <table class="matrix">
    <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Where it appeared</th></tr></thead>
    <tr><td>A tool's convenient answer accepted without checking what it actually did</td>
        <td>The silent search; the browser version assumed rather than read.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A number computed precisely from an assumption never examined</td>
        <td>The streak probability; an early measurement that reported a cost 360 times too
            high by measuring an adjacent operation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A conclusion reached from evidence gathered under different conditions</td>
        <td>Mixed host and container runs; comparing a run now against failures from an hour
            earlier, when the fault comes in streaks.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>The corrective that worked in every case was the same: change one variable, hold the rest,
  and observe the effect appear and disappear. The matched pair for the navigation fix is the
  only claim in this work that no amount of scepticism has dented, and it is the only one built
  that way.</p>
</section>
